---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'PPE and Branded Workwear Regulations for Construction Site Workers: A Las Vegas Field Guide',
 description:
  'An educational guide to PPE and branded workwear questions for Las Vegas construction crews. Employers must use a hazard assessment and current product documents.',
 category: 'Compliance',
 readTime: '12 min read',
 date: 'May 7, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-05-07',
 image: '/images/generated/kit-electrical.jpg',
 imageAlt: 'Construction crew wearing hi-vis apparel, hard hats, and branded work shirts',
 slug: 'ppe-branded-workwear-regulations-construction-las-vegas',
}

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
  {
   '@type': 'Article',
   headline: post.title,
   description: post.description,
   image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`,
   datePublished: post.isoDate,
   dateModified: '2026-08-21',
   author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/about/' },
   publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' } },
   mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
  },
  {
   '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
   itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` },
   ],
  },
  {
   '@type': 'FAQPage',
   mainEntity: [
    {
     '@type': 'Question',
     name: 'What PPE is required on a construction site under OSHA rules?',
     acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'The employer must assess the hazards of each task and select suitable PPE under the applicable OSHA rules. Project contracts and site safety plans may add requirements. Confirm every garment, rating, and standard with the employer\'s safety lead before use.',
     },
    },
    {
     '@type': 'Question',
     name: 'Can company logos be embroidered or printed on PPE without voiding the rating?',
     acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Possibly. Decoration can affect protective performance, labels, and required visible areas. The employer and garment manufacturer must approve the materials, method, and placement for the exact product before decoration.',
     },
    },
    {
     '@type': 'Question',
     name: 'Does Nevada have additional PPE rules beyond federal OSHA?',
     acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'Nevada operates an OSHA-approved state plan and has state-specific requirements. Nevada\'s heat illness regulation requires covered employers to evaluate occupational heat hazards and address identified hazards. Employers should use the current Nevada OSHA rule and guidance when building a program.',
     },
    },
    {
     '@type': 'Question',
     name: 'What hi-vis class is required for road and utility work?',
     acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'The employer selects the performance class from the hazard assessment, project rules, traffic-control plan, and current ANSI/ISEA 107 product documentation. Do not choose a class from speed, shift, or job title alone.',
     },
    },
    {
     '@type': 'Question',
     name: 'What CAT rating do FR shirts need for electrical work?',
     acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'The employer\'s electrical risk assessment determines the required arc rating and complete protective system. Voltage or job title alone does not establish the needed protection. Use current NFPA 70E guidance and the exact garment label.',
     },
    },
   ],
  },
 ],
}
---

<BaseLayout
 title={post.title}
 description={post.description}
 canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`}
 ogImage={post.image}
 type="article"
 publishedDate={post.isoDate}
>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

 <article class="bg-concrete">
  <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-20 lg:px-8">
    <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
     <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-600">Home</a>
     <span class="px-2">/</span>
     <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-600">Blog</a>
    </nav>

    <span class="kicker mb-3">{post.category}</span>
    <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-extrabold uppercase leading-tight tracking-tight text-charcoal sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">
     Use this guide to prepare questions for your safety lead. The employer's hazard assessment, current rules, and exact product documents control every selection.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
     <span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span>
     <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
     <span>{post.readTime}</span>
     <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
     <time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
    </div>
   </div>
  </header>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
   <img src={post.image} alt={post.imageAlt} width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" />
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

   <div class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6">
    <h2 class="mb-3 font-heading text-lg font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-gold-700">TL;DR</h2>
    <ul class="ml-5 list-disc space-y-2 text-base text-charcoal">
     <li>The employer's hazard assessment identifies the PPE needed for each task.</li>
     <li>Electrical, flash-fire, roadway, and other hazards can require different product ratings and complete protective systems.</li>
     <li>Nevada's <a href="/safety-compliance/nevada-osha-heat-rules-2026/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">heat illness regulation</a> adds employer obligations when occupational heat hazards are present.</li>
     <li>Decoration is not automatically acceptable. Obtain written approval for the exact garment, materials, method, and placement.</li>
    </ul>
   </div>

   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    PPE selection depends on the task, workplace, and current safety plan. This educational guide organizes the questions to take to your employer and safety lead. It is not a substitute for a hazard assessment or current regulatory advice.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">The OSHA Baseline (29 CFR 1926 Subpart E)</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    OSHA's construction rules place PPE selection and training duties on the employer. The exact requirements depend on the hazards present. Common review categories include:
   </p>
   <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li><strong>Head protection:</strong> confirm the hazard, product type, class, and employer-approved model.</li>
    <li><strong>Eye and face protection:</strong> match the protection to the task and exposure.</li>
    <li><strong>Foot protection:</strong> confirm impact, compression, electrical, puncture, and other site requirements.</li>
    <li><strong>Hearing protection:</strong> follow the employer's exposure assessment and hearing conservation program.</li>
    <li><strong>High-visibility apparel:</strong> use the class selected by the employer. See the <a href="/blog/ansi-107-class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Class 2 vs Class 3 planning guide</a>.</li>
   </ul>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Trade-Specific PPE Layers</h2>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Electrical: NFPA 70E</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Arc-rated clothing is one part of an employer-defined electrical protective system. The assessment must address the task and incident-energy exposure. Compare the required arc rating with the exact garment label. See our <a href="/blog/nfpa-70e-fr-shirts-vegas-electricians/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">FR shirt planning guide</a> and <a href="/brands/bulwark-fr-custom-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Bulwark options</a>.
   </p>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Oil, Gas, Refining: NFPA 2112</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    NFPA 2112 addresses garments intended to protect industrial personnel from short-duration thermal exposures from fire. The employer determines whether it applies. A dual-rated label does not replace separate flash-fire and electrical risk assessments.
   </p>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Roadway and Utility: ANSI/ISEA 107</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    ANSI/ISEA 107 defines performance classes for high-visibility safety apparel. The employer selects the class from the hazard assessment and applicable work-zone requirements. Verify each product's current label before ordering.
   </p>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Hand Protection (1926.95)</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Hand protection must match the task. Cut, puncture, chemical, thermal, and electrical hazards can require different products. Do not decorate protective gloves without written manufacturer and employer approval.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Nevada-Specific: The 2026 Heat Illness Rule</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Nevada operates an OSHA-approved state plan. Its heat illness regulation requires covered employers to evaluate working conditions that may cause occupational heat illness. Employers must address identified hazards under the current rule. Review the <a href="https://dir.nv.gov/OSHA/Home/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600" rel="noopener">official Nevada OSHA resources</a> and our <a href="/safety-compliance/nevada-osha-heat-rules-2026/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">educational heat guide</a>.
   </p>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Garment weight, fit, breathability, and layering can affect comfort in hot conditions. Those factors do not establish compliance or prevent heat illness. Compare employer-approved options and pair them with the employer's complete heat safety program.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Branding PPE: Questions Before Decoration</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Some protective products may be decorated. Approval depends on the exact garment and current manufacturer instructions.
   </p>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Hi-Vis (ANSI/ISEA 107)</h3>
   <ul class="mb-6 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li>Confirm the garment's required visible background and retroreflective areas.</li>
    <li>Do not cover tape, labels, or required visible areas.</li>
    <li>Use only employer-approved materials and placements permitted by the manufacturer.</li>
    <li>Keep the current garment label and any documentation required by the employer.</li>
   </ul>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">FR / Arc-Rated (NFPA 70E and 2112)</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Decoration can change a protective garment's performance. Do not assume that a thread, ink, patch, or placement is approved across brands.
   </p>
   <ul class="mb-6 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li><strong>Thread:</strong> confirm the permitted fiber and construction for the exact garment.</li>
    <li><strong>Printing:</strong> confirm the permitted ink system and application area.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfers and patches:</strong> obtain written approval for the materials, attachment method, and placement.</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> ask what product and decoration records the employer requires.</li>
   </ul>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    See the <a href="/safety-compliance/nfpa-70e-embroidery-guide/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">NFPA 70E embroidery planning guide</a> for questions to review with the employer and garment manufacturer.
   </p>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Hard Hats (ANSI Z89.1)</h3>
   <ul class="mb-6 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li>Use only markings or decals permitted by the exact hard-hat manufacturer.</li>
    <li>Do not apply unapproved paint, solvent, adhesive, or modification.</li>
    <li>Keep required labels visible and follow the employer's inspection policy.</li>
   </ul>

   <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Standard Workwear (Non-Rated)</h3>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Non-rated workwear usually allows more decoration options. Employer policies, garment care, placement, and the selected production method still apply. Compare <a href="/services/embroidery-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">embroidery</a> and <a href="/services/screen-printing-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">screen printing</a> after confirming the garment.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Your Safety Officer Actually Checks</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Ask the employer which records its safety program and contracts require. Examples may include:
   </p>
   <ol class="mb-6 ml-6 list-decimal space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li>Current product labels and manufacturer documents.</li>
    <li>Written decoration approval for the exact garment.</li>
    <li>Employee issue and training records.</li>
    <li>Inspection, care, and retirement instructions.</li>
   </ol>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    A <a href="/company-stores/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">company-store program</a> can organize an approved catalog and ordering rules. Recordkeeping, reporting, and fulfillment capabilities must be confirmed during setup.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">By Trade: Questions for the Hazard Assessment</h2>
   <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/general-contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">General contractors:</a></strong> identify traffic, falling-object, noise, eye, foot, and weather hazards.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/electrical-contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Electrical contractors:</a></strong> document shock and arc-flash risks for each task.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/plumbing-hvac-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Plumbing, HVAC, and mechanical:</a></strong> assess hot work, chemicals, energized equipment, and active traffic zones.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/roofing-solar-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Roofing and solar:</a></strong> assess fall, electrical, heat, and sun exposure.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/data-center-contractors-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Data center contractors:</a></strong> follow the site plan and task-specific electrical requirements.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/concrete-masonry-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Concrete and masonry:</a></strong> assess noise, silica, chemical, hand, knee, eye, and foot hazards.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/industries/landscaping-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Landscaping:</a></strong> assess traffic, heat, noise, eye, chemical, and equipment hazards.</li>
   </ul>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Where Decoration Programs Go Wrong</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Two decoration risks deserve written review:
   </p>
   <ul class="mb-6 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li><strong>Unapproved materials on protective garments.</strong> Thread, backing, ink, adhesive, or patches may affect performance.</li>
    <li><strong>Decoration over required visible areas.</strong> A logo may reduce background or retroreflective area.</li>
   </ul>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    Your employer and garment manufacturer must approve the decoration method, materials, and placement. Bighorn coordinates the written requirements with the selected production partner. Documentation availability depends on the manufacturer and partner.
   </p>

   <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">References (the standards themselves)</h2>
   <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-base leading-relaxed text-ash">
    <li>OSHA 29 CFR 1926 Subpart E — Personal Protective and Life Saving Equipment</li>
    <li>ANSI/ISEA 107 — High-Visibility Safety Apparel</li>
    <li>ANSI/ISEA Z89.1 — Industrial Head Protection</li>
    <li>ANSI Z87.1 — Eye and Face Protection</li>
    <li>ASTM F2413 — Standard Specification for Performance Requirements for Protective Footwear</li>
    <li>NFPA 70E — Standard for Electrical Safety in the Workplace</li>
    <li>NFPA 2112 — Standard on Flame-Resistant Clothing for Protection of Industrial Personnel Against Short-Duration Thermal Exposures from Fire</li>
    <li>Nevada OSHA — adopted federal standards plus state-specific heat illness prevention rule (2026)</li>
   </ul>

   <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-8">
    <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-extrabold uppercase tracking-tight text-charcoal">Planning branded PPE for a crew?</h3>
    <p class="mb-6 text-lg text-ash">
     Bighorn coordinates a current review of the exact protective garment, decoration, placement, and manufacturer instructions with the selected production partner. Talk to us about your program.
    </p>
    <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="btn-primary text-base">Request a PPE Program Quote</a>
   </div>
  </div>
 </article>
</BaseLayout>
